[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527945 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a403057-49da-5281-95e9-2b61e1f6c156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(4,2,2)$ and $(8,4,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527579} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc1d652-8f64-55d9-a0a5-6794fcdf58b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(4,2,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527586} {:answer/id #uuid "a19965fa-cbd9-5c65-bf6b-ca13371cc307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3})$ and $(4,2,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527640} {:answer/id #uuid "ce32204a-bd90-5a02-95bb-9e251b7948af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(8,4,3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527726} {:answer/id #uuid "d755c9c6-8859-50b7-b00f-25201a9e2e8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3})$ and $(8,4,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a19965fa-cbd9-5c65-bf6b-ca13371cc307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3})$ and $(4,2,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527640} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b76cfe8-40cb-5f07-be72-0076c0000916" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a9b6c0a-2d84-570a-8860-6a6e61ceeed3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94084" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the elliptic cone ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=8{z}^{2}$ and the line $\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{2}=z-1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{2}=z-1=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4t \\\\ y=2t \\\\ z=t+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2} & =8{z}^{2} \\\\ (4t)^{2}+4(2t)^{2} & =8(t+1)^{2} \\\\ 16{t}^{2}+16{t}^{2} & =8(t+1)^{2} \\\\ 32{t}^{2} & =8(t+1)^{2} \\\\ 4{t}^{2} & =(t+1)^{2} \\\\ 4{t}^{2} & ={t}^{2}+2t+1 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-2t-1 & =0 \\\\ (3t+1)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{3},1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4(-\\frac{1}{3})=-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ y=2(-\\frac{1}{3})=-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ z=-\\frac{1}{3}+1=\\frac{2}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(-\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3}).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4(1)=4 \\\\ y=2(1)=2 \\\\ z=1+1=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(4,2,2).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3})$ and $(4,2,2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0903858d-cf16-597b-9ae8-d85d0bc36245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-4)$ in the $yz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527324} {:answer/id #uuid "3902c414-780f-5eef-ba7d-8eda7aba1b47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,-2)$ in the $yz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527420} {:answer/id #uuid "78287034-4646-5978-adb5-dcdbf0469d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ in the $yz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527531} {:answer/id #uuid "ab75c7e1-d61a-56b5-b8e3-f5fa5dd5aece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,-4)$ in the $yz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527660} {:answer/id #uuid "c6455024-0d8f-5fa3-83e1-94779ed93f71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,4)$ in the $yz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab75c7e1-d61a-56b5-b8e3-f5fa5dd5aece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,-4)$ in the $yz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527660} :answer-field/id #uuid "f913ee15-c6e1-5a0b-bf56-708c893775f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65227920-db32-5f4f-9774-747e0bc8ffd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-73867" :question/problem "For the elliptic paraboloid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{3}=z+4$, the section in the plane $\\sqrt{3}x-2y=4$ is a parabola. \nFind the vertex of the projection of this parabola onto $yz$-plane." :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}x-2y=4\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2(y+2)}{\\sqrt{3}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{(\\frac{2(y+2)}{\\sqrt{3}})}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{3} & =z+4 \\\\ \\frac{4(y+2)^{2}}{3\\cdot 2}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{3} & =z+4 \\\\ \\frac{2(y+2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{3} & =z+4 \\\\ \\frac{3(y+2)^{2}}{3} & =z+4 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =z+4 \\\\ z & =(y+2)^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the vertex of the projection onto $yz$-plane is $(-2,-4).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10b3f812-017d-59eb-99e5-5a00f74bf7d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527344} {:answer/id #uuid "35e1c9bf-ea2b-560e-8f70-12de88c73f78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527418} {:answer/id #uuid "49e17673-b9ad-517c-bb9b-8d3f2668d8ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527452} {:answer/id #uuid "78e4424f-a2d2-5162-a013-91916d289fbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527534} {:answer/id #uuid "e438768b-4435-5fed-b94f-351a63a4645d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78e4424f-a2d2-5162-a013-91916d289fbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527534} :answer-field/id #uuid "3888cc3d-bb90-568c-bfd5-048cece1217d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a83145c-370c-5913-b3c6-439072b6c354" :question/math-academy-id "q-161772" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}xyz\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the type III region given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{4-2z},\\,0\\le y\\le x\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}xyz\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-2x}}{\\int }_{0}^{x}xyz\\,dy\\,dx\\,dz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-2x}}{\\int }_{0}^{x}xyz\\,dy\\,dx\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-2x}}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}xyz\\,dy]dx\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-2x}}xz{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}dx\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-2x}}xz(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})\\,dx\\,dz \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-2x}}{x}^{3}z\\,dx\\,dz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x$, treating $z$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-2x}}{x}^{3}z\\,dx\\,dz & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}z[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-2z}}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}z{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{4-2z}}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}(4-2z)^{2}z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4z-4{z}^{2}+{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4z-4{z}^{2}+{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{1}{2}{[2{z}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}{z}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{4}]}_{z=0}^{z=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(8-\\frac{32}{3}+4)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527958 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "058cac9d-5a5a-5ee7-9198-6ebfb7207e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({[5-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{2}{y}+2]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527319} {:answer/id #uuid "7023af54-631a-5dcf-bf27-5f2705dfb93d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({[3-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{2}{y}]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527516} {:answer/id #uuid "80f6d32e-5475-5dfc-9839-a9615e457aa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({[2+\\frac{2}{y}]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527559} {:answer/id #uuid "d10abc45-5b7c-5d5e-9462-2d886e2f6bc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({[5-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{2}{y}+2]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527730} {:answer/id #uuid "d30b7d3d-0dea-5470-83f6-553983d20016" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}({[5-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{2}{y}+2]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527733}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d10abc45-5b7c-5d5e-9462-2d886e2f6bc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({[5-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{2}{y}+2]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527730} :answer-field/id #uuid "24a6b34c-6b2b-59cf-97a5-55f1d3c4912c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7bcaaf90-d33e-5a2f-9583-78877f436c71" :question/math-academy-id "q-50330" :question/problem "![](images/31/31d4236bf58cd579c49c7259c32a2a9171e07c771b4c34040ac92896809f695f.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x=\\frac{2}{y}$ and $x=3-y$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=-2.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{y} & =3-y \\\\ 2 & =3y-{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-3y+2 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y-2) & =0 \\\\ y & =1,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=1$ to $y=2.$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/b7/b73c341f4d09b1df7bea162bc7dbe10d0ee60920c847f9c24210386a3d139e61.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=\\frac{2}{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=3-y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=3-y.$\n\n![](images/c8/c8c8e766c66e47a54f316f54893d603b245acec8f529da3a5033d2bba547a319.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(3-y)-(-2)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[5-y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=\\frac{2}{y}.$\n\n![](images/1b/1b31331f1c0da353bee6fe29d10e9e58dd531cbc8152cbce214d1adea771b032.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\,(\\frac{2}{y})-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\frac{2}{y}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[5-y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\frac{2}{y}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({[5-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{2}{y}+2]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527959 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0091f92f-76bd-5455-b7bb-90620581c533" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527308} {:answer/id #uuid "4faca5a4-1b5a-5d74-9628-f54841efcefa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527461} {:answer/id #uuid "7fbb2c06-77c6-5ca1-ad81-f51fd4aa46f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527553} {:answer/id #uuid "93f7fcfc-7f4c-5178-9ad6-2289d08312cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527601} {:answer/id #uuid "97a20052-f025-5e71-8ba6-be7ff103ec85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97a20052-f025-5e71-8ba6-be7ff103ec85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527613} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3ee0695-4508-5b1d-af93-79c7b38f9ec9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7bdb766f-cf3d-53c5-a841-7b6efda706a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-28653" :question/problem "$5{cos}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}+3{sin}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}-7{tan}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}=$" :question/worked-solution "The angle ${45}^{∘}$ is special, and its trigonometric ratios have the values\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}=1,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nAlso, note that ${sin}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}$ means $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2},$ ${cos}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}$ means $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2}$ and  ${tan}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}$ means $(\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{cos}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}+3{sin}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}-7{tan}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘} &= 5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2}+3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2}-7(\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2} \\\\ &= 5{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}+3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}-7(1)^{2} \\\\ &= 8{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}-7 \\\\ &= 8(\\frac{2}{4})-7 \\\\ &= 4-7 \\\\ &= -3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1096e9cc-0d05-54f0-a8b0-b4c0608e0b12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527341} {:answer/id #uuid "58d0b037-6774-52f7-93e7-6f3ae6d9c7cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527478} {:answer/id #uuid "ae9a381d-5fac-5694-95d2-991e04d47b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527665} {:answer/id #uuid "ee741f96-ad03-5303-910e-ae71eb5a15f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527768} {:answer/id #uuid "f2abc025-91c6-53cf-babe-651b78bd05b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58d0b037-6774-52f7-93e7-6f3ae6d9c7cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527478} :answer-field/id #uuid "90de2426-d3b0-54f0-abb8-0e8cf9780d48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7e4093f7-c4fd-5107-9969-358e958506e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-146272" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{3}{16}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=4]$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|4}=\\frac{4}{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ let's first find the marginal distribution of $Y,$ corresponding to the column totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{3}{16}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{5}{16}$ | $\\frac{11}{16}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding column total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y:$\n\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{3}{11}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{4}{5}$ | $\\frac{8}{11}$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=4] & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)]-{\\mu }_{X|4}^{2} \\\\  & =[{0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}+{1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}]-{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d9012e4-4080-5eed-b0f1-ea73de461ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d9012e4-4080-5eed-b0f1-ea73de461ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527374} :answer-field/id #uuid "23614fcf-fbe4-5be9-ae9e-34482a74c5f3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527812} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c25e94b-079d-51a9-bf98-4849a2d4378b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c25e94b-079d-51a9-bf98-4849a2d4378b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527369} :answer-field/id #uuid "298c6150-0d93-5585-9a25-200dda26c396" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8516a769-888c-5185-902e-1a5250ccaa04" :question/math-academy-id "q-350393" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0<t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=3\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m:$\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=\\sqrt{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\sqrt{3}(x-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\sqrt{3}x-\\frac{3(3)}{2} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{3}x-\\frac{9}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{3}x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at  $(0,\\,-4).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527993 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18c82db0-9172-53d3-850a-85947c46a5d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18c82db0-9172-53d3-850a-85947c46a5d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527358} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc7e3261-08d2-5e99-8825-f84a4ffcfbd5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527907} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e67e0c17-5d27-54f5-a8e6-a2baf1d7260c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e67e0c17-5d27-54f5-a8e6-a2baf1d7260c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527761} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff55a139-4c0c-56e8-a3f1-ea5a3e41bb14" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cdc93f1c-a598-5c34-91ec-6e0adc895b1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-350343" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}t+\\frac{3}{2},\\,y={t}^{3}+7{t}^{2}+8t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=-1$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(-1)+\\frac{3}{2}=1-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}=2 \\\\ y & =(-1)^{3}+7(-1)^{2}+8(-1)=-1+7-8=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(2,-2).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+14t+8\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{t}^{2}+14t+8}{2t+\\frac{1}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,-1} & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}+14(-1)+8}{2(-1)+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,-2)$ and the slope $m=2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-2) & =2(x-2) \\\\ y+2 & =2x-4 \\\\ y & =2x-4-2 \\\\ y & =2x-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2(0)-6 \\\\ y & =0-6 \\\\ y & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at  $(0,\\,-6).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a76db44-e626-54e3-bb32-fdade55a01be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527402} {:answer/id #uuid "35321656-4bd6-5396-805d-37ad3b680477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527416} {:answer/id #uuid "719b0fe5-3c99-5cac-84f5-1fc289687dce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527521} {:answer/id #uuid "c50c3670-73c7-5cc3-9ccf-673bb3533570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527701} {:answer/id #uuid "c52c231c-1dfb-55b4-9960-d0a4177ddef9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a76db44-e626-54e3-bb32-fdade55a01be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527402} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb85d00d-c4fb-5b35-911e-a717db9a9969" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dc80cc6c-733b-50ec-85a0-e9f5b6f29bef" :question/math-academy-id "q-73332" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={x}^{2}-1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,x\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $0$ | $3$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3})|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & ={\\frac{{u}^{3/2}}{3}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{3/2}}{3}-\\frac{{0}^{3/2}}{3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528012 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51e8fb2f-fb5d-5f28-bb8f-bf604bccc7dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51e8fb2f-fb5d-5f28-bb8f-bf604bccc7dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527464} :answer-field/id #uuid "0560cfb9-c1b9-5465-8ef1-a2f3a5215585" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f4bbe9de-e1d4-5432-b7cd-e16cf2a892fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-352604" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n}\\sqrt{n}{(\\frac{x}{6})}^{n}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$\n\nThe radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}\\sqrt{n+1}{(\\frac{x}{6})}^{n+1}}{(-1)^{n}\\sqrt{n}{(\\frac{x}{6})}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\sqrt{n+1}}{\\sqrt{n}}||\\frac{x}{6}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|x|}{6} & <1 \\\\ |x| & <6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is $R=6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528013 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "081ac015-24cb-5663-8688-ae1b7ff7348b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{4}{\\,e\\,}x+\\frac{2{e}^{2}-4}{\\,e\\,}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527322} {:answer/id #uuid "434c5620-db8a-5f85-858a-d1decc931c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{\\,e\\,}x+\\frac{4{e}^{2}-2}{\\,e\\,}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527437} {:answer/id #uuid "a8cc7b58-1e78-5215-ae55-4b924f699a54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{\\,e\\,}x-\\frac{2{e}^{2}-4}{\\,e\\,}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527653} {:answer/id #uuid "b63fdc98-6ffe-5091-898f-611ee1b4b7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}x+\\frac{2{e}^{2}+4}{\\,e\\,}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527679} {:answer/id #uuid "cd19d4c1-53f7-5df5-ae10-f2400625057c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}x+\\frac{2{e}^{2}-4}{\\,e\\,}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b63fdc98-6ffe-5091-898f-611ee1b4b7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}x+\\frac{2{e}^{2}+4}{\\,e\\,}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527679} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac112813-4b74-55f0-8f43-22f46db91806" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f508d724-e816-5421-91b6-b8baa491ba13" :question/math-academy-id "q-122928" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,y=2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,0<t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=e$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=e$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=4, \\\\ y & =2e\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=2e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(4,2e).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{4}{t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2}{(\\frac{4}{t})} \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=e,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=e} & =\\frac{e\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+e}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{e+e}{2}=e\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(4,2e)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2e & =(-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,})(x-4) \\\\ y-2e & =-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}x+\\frac{4}{\\,e\\,} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}x+\\frac{2{e}^{2}+4}{\\,e\\,}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528284 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72c06151-b0bc-5bf7-89e6-7cd048213cf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=- \\frac{83}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528243} {:answer/id #uuid "7b937b79-1192-5163-9942-a0d3fa745aa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=- \\frac{127}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528245} {:answer/id #uuid "b79c8f65-af57-5c16-953f-ade51714756f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=- \\frac{13}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528267} {:answer/id #uuid "d0c10f58-fb66-5a44-a714-46c874b40ffe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=- \\frac{22}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528275} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f43626-2bd8-5da8-b374-7ec6593b0bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=- \\frac{127}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1f43626-2bd8-5da8-b374-7ec6593b0bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=- \\frac{127}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528283} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e0ff4da-3687-5c2f-a149-9fe8a300e152" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e4ff63f2-f505-569f-8024-44d95016c5b9" :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed sine function with a minimum value of $- \\frac{83}{6}$ and a maximum value of $- \\frac{22}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "A sinusoidal graph's midline (or vertical shift) is the horizontal line that divides it vertically in half. \n\nThe equation of the midline is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{\\text{min}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{max}}$ are the minimum and maximum values of the function. The midline equals the average of the maximum and minimum values. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2} & =\\frac{- \\frac{83}{6}+(- \\frac{22}{3})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{-83}{6}+(\\frac{-44}{6})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{- \\frac{127}{6}}{2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{127}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=- \\frac{127}{12}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529093 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bb127fe-81a1-51d8-a7f3-7f6901827eb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528375} {:answer/id #uuid "3574038e-055d-56a6-9f8a-f1deedf83863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528434} {:answer/id #uuid "359b2829-aef3-5244-a711-6b233756f5c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{23}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528436} {:answer/id #uuid "75aed8b7-b806-509d-904b-d5e5a1544691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528572} {:answer/id #uuid "ad2bcd84-ba3a-519d-a0e6-d8c907296ffc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{27}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75aed8b7-b806-509d-904b-d5e5a1544691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528572} :answer-field/id #uuid "4409dd34-9ab8-5a98-a1d2-9602397921b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14f76056-5e3c-539d-8f82-855878eb07d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-50498" :question/problem "Given that ${a}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-\\frac{1}{10}$ for all integers $n\\ge 1,$ find the value of ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is geometric series because the ratio of any two consecutive terms is constant.\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nWe're told that the first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{5}{2}}{(1-(-\\frac{1}{10}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{5}{2}}{(\\frac{11}{10})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{10}{11} \\\\  & =-\\frac{25}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529095 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48706475-0664-565e-bd2d-7f83441bc4d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528481} {:answer/id #uuid "99d1f1a0-32aa-5964-8ea7-584cb8f97f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528665} {:answer/id #uuid "c448d435-9bcd-595a-9b15-481488adc3cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528780} {:answer/id #uuid "c948edf4-7c1a-595a-9006-e62d1d1f4937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528793} {:answer/id #uuid "f7aa2104-1ab4-5d6d-8862-d71d4c61233f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c448d435-9bcd-595a-9b15-481488adc3cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528780} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c25b804-adcd-5109-97e9-a671522d2fa7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "17abd4d5-764f-50c1-82c3-4fcec4b8a574" :question/math-academy-id "q-122546" :question/problem "![](images/8c/8ce8a8061ec087c39ce93d73d0566832f33c4e6ce5c2f5185170c9372a0d88fa.png)\n\nConsider the finite region above bounded by $y=2x+2$ and $y=\\sqrt{4x+4},$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x\\text{-axis.}$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2x+2$ and $g(x)=\\sqrt{4x+4}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 2x+2 & =\\sqrt{4x+4} \\\\ 2(x+1) & =\\sqrt{4x+4} \\\\ 4(x+1)^{2} & =4x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1 & =x+1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x & =0 \\\\ x(x+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=-1$ to $x=0.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/0a/0a6bdcc071500ac8dd40143c3219a14c80fc73d543d7307cb2c880a22db793a3.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=f(x)=2x+2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=g(x)=\\sqrt{4x+4}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}({[\\sqrt{4x+4}]}^{2}-{[2x+2]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}[4x+4-(2(x+1))^{2}]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}[4(x+1)-4({x}^{2}+2x+1)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}[x+1-({x}^{2}+2x+1)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}(-{x}^{2}-x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {(-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =4\\pi (0-(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =4\\pi (\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529112 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "773ffc42-e2cb-572a-9030-e9a10ba881ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi +24}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528576} {:answer/id #uuid "a1bcdf62-382b-5121-8803-109ccdad8245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi -9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528689} {:answer/id #uuid "a5649c91-b6ce-5e11-a61e-4473ca3b44bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27\\pi -9}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528700} {:answer/id #uuid "a8b16dc0-ef14-5998-aff3-022fa9ad94bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi +72}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528711} {:answer/id #uuid "b1addd5e-b739-5991-8970-6b78acd4e746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27\\pi -72}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1addd5e-b739-5991-8970-6b78acd4e746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27\\pi -72}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528732} :answer-field/id #uuid "870af8e4-9463-5142-8458-8ef364ddaa4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c3c2375-fac6-5465-bbbf-42c39eaebb70" :question/math-academy-id "q-123912" :question/problem "![](images/98/98bc6f2ea4d71368226f0f784d629e98949e07005bc08158206de35a87bea520.png)\n\nFind the area bounded by the polar curve $r=3-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=3-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=2\\pi $, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(3-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(9-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}\\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi })) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{4}\\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4}\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{8}\\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4}(2\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-9(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}))+\\frac{9}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{27\\pi }{8}-9+\\frac{9}{8}(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{27\\pi -72}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529118 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04935b22-0a1e-541f-a73a-987f30ee4b47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528311} {:answer/id #uuid "1a25cb22-7e63-590c-9899-23cbef8fefce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528368} {:answer/id #uuid "2cbb7959-4b4b-548e-9199-2fcffd9bfefb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528414} {:answer/id #uuid "aaca8c15-d6ca-5c02-bf7f-dcb675eef426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528716} {:answer/id #uuid "f008eb8a-552e-5329-9ff3-06c4e1005ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a25cb22-7e63-590c-9899-23cbef8fefce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528368} :answer-field/id #uuid "0aaf7a48-2de5-5bcf-abf9-e9ddfe97ac48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "499dc119-15d3-5a33-9631-8bc1b2a65d1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-5362" :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2n}.$" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nThe first term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2(1)}=\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the second term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2(2)}=\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{4}}{\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}}={(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}=\\frac{4}{25}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}}{1-\\frac{4}{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{7}{10}(\\frac{4}{25})}{\\frac{21}{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10}\\cdot \\frac{4}{25}\\cdot \\frac{25}{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529124 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "976b5925-40fb-5125-b552-7a9d7cec7fe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "976b5925-40fb-5125-b552-7a9d7cec7fe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528659} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cde64eb-fc8f-574e-a4b3-4a914b9a4d5e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54f15411-79a5-546c-8ee4-965cb1e88cd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-350607" :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $4.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{4},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{4}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}=\\frac{16}{81} \\\\ {a}_{5} & ={(\\frac{2}{3})}^{5}=\\frac{32}{243}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{4}}=\\frac{(\\frac{32}{243})}{(\\frac{16}{81})}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{4}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{16}{81})}{(1-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{16}{81})}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529125 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df9bedc8-89b3-5ab1-b0b3-a9b2ac3b4951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df9bedc8-89b3-5ab1-b0b3-a9b2ac3b4951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528847} :answer-field/id #uuid "d13a95c4-8252-5e9e-aaed-c7750144cf3c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56e0e8e1-0cfd-5d25-b2ed-327a18d60529" :question/math-academy-id "q-350582" :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=8{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{1}=3.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{8{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{2}}{8{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{1}}=\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(1-\\frac{3}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(\\frac{5}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc33e925-2bf6-51a9-b4d5-52ab4d837e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc33e925-2bf6-51a9-b4d5-52ab4d837e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528929} :answer-field/id #uuid "77d5f4a7-be7e-5df2-a989-37fce15016eb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c0c2f91-9457-576f-ab5b-77c8f4a530ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-350579" :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=4{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{1}=\\frac{12}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{4{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}}{4{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{1}}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{12}{5})}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{12}{5})}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "052804b5-ac12-5294-a262-e3fa2b590da2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528315} {:answer/id #uuid "31321875-45ba-5d60-9460-76f9ff4b65bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528422} {:answer/id #uuid "66deb24d-93e5-58be-9017-317a664a99a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528546} {:answer/id #uuid "c902eb5b-0235-5da3-b574-a9735354dbe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528792} {:answer/id #uuid "d7217da2-ae78-508a-874f-f889c1b07a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7217da2-ae78-508a-874f-f889c1b07a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528836} :answer-field/id #uuid "52b89aa0-9d44-5e08-ab59-3cc05bb04a17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "72ca9ca6-7469-59a8-9b29-546c35450d5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-146353" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2+y-x}{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5-2x}{6}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]$ is a function of $x.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2+y-x}{3})}{(\\frac{5-2x}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2+y-x)}{5-2x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=0.$\n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ into the expression for ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)=\\frac{2(2+y-1)}{5-2\\cdot 1}=\\frac{2(1+y)}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot \\frac{2(1+y)}{3}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y+{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bdef5ee-d3f3-562e-aecc-41aeb9ad117d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528330} {:answer/id #uuid "0f2772e5-3a67-5a55-9efd-47b34d382cca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528334} {:answer/id #uuid "2105d49c-3838-5c6f-a36c-1a555b26bcd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528392} {:answer/id #uuid "25c78422-62a4-52b1-bc72-b3de2ce6a592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528398} {:answer/id #uuid "2a9324da-a375-57dd-a2cb-31842855799b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bdef5ee-d3f3-562e-aecc-41aeb9ad117d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528330} :answer-field/id #uuid "c15c1c6b-8e24-5b94-b478-74191ff0b0e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75a391e4-92f0-5306-8519-d804235fa403" :question/math-academy-id "q-78591" :question/problem "Evaluate the series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $2.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{2},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}=\\frac{16}{25} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{64}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{64}{125})}{(\\frac{16}{25})}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{16}{25})}{(1-\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{16}{25})}{(\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529147 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1ffe733-727d-5ca8-9de6-2f0a4144bd80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1ffe733-727d-5ca8-9de6-2f0a4144bd80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528896} :answer-field/id #uuid "451b0468-1ab6-5622-abf5-d01927a565b8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "858e5587-42fe-5f2f-94b7-94758f1a2164" :question/math-academy-id "q-348315" :question/problem "![](images/f3/f33fde62793530cfe8970a9fea690fc71036312483ea617088a8d0a663d5133a.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ bounded by the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )$, ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =(\\theta +\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta )){|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}))+\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529149 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0b39530-de5a-5c77-986a-201fc9682d01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0b39530-de5a-5c77-986a-201fc9682d01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528730} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d7cde26-15d1-5ea3-9851-e82954572829" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "86ea583e-6e3e-5bb4-93ab-14ccfe18681c" :question/math-academy-id "q-352297" :question/problem "Find the area $A$ bounded by the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))+\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\pi +0 \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529160 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0fa857c-1d3e-5cf2-baa1-2ef2c08c36f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17\\pi}{3}+14\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0fa857c-1d3e-5cf2-baa1-2ef2c08c36f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17\\pi}{3}+14\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528851} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea42cbea-59a6-5df3-b3cd-da64d32d78f3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "acc4627b-a75b-5710-8130-818c19bd3eef" :question/math-academy-id "q-352321" :question/problem "Find the area $A$ bounded by the polar curve $r=3+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=3+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}(3+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}(9+24\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +16{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}(9+24\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +8(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(17\\theta +24\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )){|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(17\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}+24\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{2}\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})+12(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}))+2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\pi }{3}+12(\\sqrt{3})+2(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\pi }{3}+14\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529166 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02c49c4a-231f-5e98-b371-0e935a20a3ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528305} {:answer/id #uuid "92fa2249-5af2-5ed4-b4f5-e80a0891b31b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528650} {:answer/id #uuid "a59e2126-217b-588b-8ec1-c18e748c0160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528701} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e58acf-da9e-5137-9d01-db3f88e60768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528788} {:answer/id #uuid "d7d6410d-8ce9-57d1-9928-c24c78a23343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7d6410d-8ce9-57d1-9928-c24c78a23343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528837} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0a449ce-c8fa-55c9-ab8d-6d9f7f6b2000" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c0901d8a-5ab9-5481-aeea-fb9811603032" :question/math-academy-id "q-146256" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(x+y)}{4}, & 0<x\\le 2y,\\,\\,0<y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}, & 0<y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $Y$ are shown above. Find the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=1].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]$ is a function of $y.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3(x+y)}{4})}{3{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+y)}{4{y}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<x\\le 2y,\\,0<y\\le 1.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=0.$ \n\nNow, substituting $y=1$ into the expression for ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)=\\frac{(x+1)}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{(x+1)}{4}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}+x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(\\frac{8}{3}+2)-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529175 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12bfe273-020f-5d83-9faf-6cff62334849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528345} {:answer/id #uuid "2c31661b-2050-5fa7-bf5f-9b3a7ef311ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528411} {:answer/id #uuid "2c61ae29-cae7-5ccc-a372-b73c4206fcc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528412} {:answer/id #uuid "8559fe88-e67b-5f1b-bf3d-bbf3de7441ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528617} {:answer/id #uuid "94cc8691-117b-5651-83f2-f780d14663d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c61ae29-cae7-5ccc-a372-b73c4206fcc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528412} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec27395b-38a3-58fa-99e7-8f30382149a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d588b5a8-8a0c-5e4d-8e17-d8c93ee42445" :question/math-academy-id "q-78592" :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $2.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{2},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{9}{25})=\\frac{3}{25} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{27}{125})=\\frac{9}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{9}{125})}{(\\frac{3}{25})}=\\frac{9}{125}\\cdot \\frac{25}{3}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{25})}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{25})}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529177 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c83586b2-1d92-5d5e-8f6e-4972aca897e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c83586b2-1d92-5d5e-8f6e-4972aca897e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528789} :answer-field/id #uuid "55196b73-4bdb-541d-9f20-8e0ffb0bc727" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d870d48d-9aa5-5918-85fa-03f4b0df3775" :question/math-academy-id "q-352336" :question/problem "Find the area $A$ bounded by the polar curve $r=2\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(2\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4{\\theta }^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\theta }^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2(\\frac{{\\theta }^{3}}{3}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{(\\pi /2)^{3}}{3})-2(\\frac{{0}^{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529180 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ad32293-81c4-5632-9b56-dce7d9c78668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528328} {:answer/id #uuid "3bdb6d77-f65e-5ff5-becb-dd2f3d35c069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528453} {:answer/id #uuid "5c4e7ff3-16b6-51f5-a98c-6ce0c3972663" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528518} {:answer/id #uuid "843d0574-e70b-5e22-b58c-6a661198e3c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528614} {:answer/id #uuid "b2433a72-ac85-5f1f-b794-39e122d2d8d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bdb6d77-f65e-5ff5-becb-dd2f3d35c069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528453} :answer-field/id #uuid "5101b7ce-f6ad-523b-93e4-83e6665f500b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd479934-e321-5035-bf71-51bd7984a9a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-124588" :question/problem "Evaluate the series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }180{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $2.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{2},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & =180{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}=5 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =180{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{3}=\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{5}{6})}{5}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }180{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(1-\\frac{1}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(\\frac{5}{6})} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529182 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "070e71d3-4fa0-5f24-95c6-4392df98055f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528321} {:answer/id #uuid "358ce659-62c7-5b50-8b6a-72ee4e8e55df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528435} {:answer/id #uuid "3ec18b62-e688-5729-8acb-74067dce2afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528461} {:answer/id #uuid "b575e07e-689b-50db-9fe4-49b41ddcdf6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528744} {:answer/id #uuid "e072663c-1824-5e1b-a41f-e8be263083ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e072663c-1824-5e1b-a41f-e8be263083ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528848} :answer-field/id #uuid "160bbd50-69e6-5813-9152-8fb571501011" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df45f3a3-f545-5c42-98d5-edf671e88e36" :question/math-academy-id "q-124584" :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n}=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=10{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{1}=6.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{10{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}}{10{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{1}}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529194 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a3480c0-71b3-53a7-8e12-a5f45e9dc984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528369} {:answer/id #uuid "410edf4d-43bb-535f-8576-68374334c4b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528467} {:answer/id #uuid "9a0391d6-60ff-509f-9126-d08627def1cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528666} {:answer/id #uuid "d808212c-d12b-5613-8018-d3e5a2a5ce89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528839} {:answer/id #uuid "fc743163-b80f-5adb-9a55-ae315a62a76c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc743163-b80f-5adb-9a55-ae315a62a76c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528930} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3c44b61-550f-50e7-828e-641fd371090c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fbd76d75-8ee7-5bcd-acb2-1f5ae9aa3787" :question/math-academy-id "q-67301" :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n}=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=8{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{1}=6.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{8{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}}{8{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{1}}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(1-\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529209 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bf30a48-c9e1-56ae-a6e7-d1c98a8ff3ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529217} {:answer/id #uuid "c73dcc5f-2dfd-545e-8686-1192c87baf69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bf30a48-c9e1-56ae-a6e7-d1c98a8ff3ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529217} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c836807-d3ab-5654-828e-8cfc6899a48e" :answer-field/key "$Q$ is **not** a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529226} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3352e3a8-a31e-5489-b928-3600c98b31be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529215} {:answer/id #uuid "7eea3389-ae38-5700-acdf-02838bc43de5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7eea3389-ae38-5700-acdf-02838bc43de5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529233} :answer-field/id #uuid "7319f1b7-7a37-5090-bc1d-79d9eee3c8d9" :answer-field/key "$det(Q)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529231} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59fb7559-abec-5a5a-8f38-66e0e05169a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529221} {:answer/id #uuid "edd1215a-893f-52eb-9b40-de5e574e65b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edd1215a-893f-52eb-9b40-de5e574e65b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529249} :answer-field/id #uuid "885ed53f-bd5b-5462-9002-72b92ffeea39" :answer-field/key "$Q$ is a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0f86b247-8f09-549d-8811-1a1520e150f7" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $Q$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & -1 & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & 2 & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & -1 & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & 2 & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 2 & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}- \\sqrt{2})+1(- \\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{3})+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}+4 \\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\cdot (- \\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2})+1\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{16 \\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{20}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(Q)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$Q$ is a singular matrix” and $det(Q)=0$ are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529214 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "623acce1-fff7-50e6-838b-7b0788c7f4cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529223} {:answer/id #uuid "6f989a4f-56c1-50c2-8a6c-3fc3965f7ada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text}